WEBVTT

00:00.450 --> 00:04.790
You've just looked at this graph and picked a main story.

00:05.620 --> 00:08.630
Let's see if you found the same one I did.

00:10.100 --> 00:14.620
A line graph shows how something changes, usually over time.

00:15.000 --> 00:17.480
Each line is one thing being measured.

00:18.300 --> 00:23.310
Here we have two: people reading the news online,
and people reading it in print.

00:24.800 --> 00:26.700
So what's the story?

00:26.900 --> 00:34.500
Online reading went up, from 18% of adults in 2004
to 74% in 2024.

00:35.400 --> 00:40.190
Print went the other way, down from 64% to 26%.

00:41.450 --> 00:43.550
And here's the moment that matters most.

00:43.550 --> 00:49.600
Somewhere between 2012 and 2016, the lines cross.

00:49.700 --> 00:51.890
Online overtook print.

00:52.950 --> 00:55.600
When I was an examiner, this is what I looked for.

00:55.600 --> 01:01.100
If a candidate noticed the crossover, I knew they'd read
the whole graph, not just one number.

01:01.200 --> 01:06.450
A sentence like "In 2008, 30% read online" is true,

01:06.850 --> 01:08.000
but it doesn't tell the story.

01:09.000 --> 01:12.950
Remember, you describe what happened.
You don't explain why.

01:13.550 --> 01:17.700
Now it's your turn. On the right page,
you'll find a weak sentence:

01:17.750 --> 01:19.900
"The online number went up a lot."

01:20.000 --> 01:21.550
Make it precise.

01:21.600 --> 01:27.250
Say what was measured, use a strong verb,
and give the figures and the years.
